<Window xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib"
        Title="respawnkeeper console"
        Height="1032" Width="1880" MinHeight="870" MinWidth="1480"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
        Background="#121212"
        TextOptions.TextFormattingMode="Ideal"
        UseLayoutRounding="True">

  <!--
    THE SERVER CONSOLE.

    Minecraft is launched with `nogui`, so its own Swing window never appears and
    the only face the server has is a cmd window. This is the replacement for
    that face - not for the supervisor's own console, which keeps printing what
    respawnkeeper itself is doing.

    IT DOES NOT OWN THE SERVER. It reads logs\latest.log and writes command
    files that the supervisor picks up. Closing this window does nothing to the
    server; opening it while the server is already up works; opening two of them
    works. A console that owned the process would mean "closed the window" and
    "killed the server" were the same gesture, which is the accident this whole
    project exists to prevent.

    THE STAGE IS NEVER SCALED. It is drawn at exactly the zoom stage.json asks
    for (560x376 at scale 2 = 1120x752) and nothing in this file or in
    rk-console.ps1 is allowed to shrink it. It is nearest-neighbour pixel art,
    and a non-integer fit - 0.75 is what stood here until 2026-09-10 - turns
    every 2px feature into an uneven mix of one and two screen pixels. The eyes
    are going to become a high-detail layer with an animation inside it, and
    every screen pixel spent on them has to be a whole one.

    So the room the stage needs comes out of the CHROME instead: the nine
    buttons, the header, and the gaps between them all shrink with the window,
    down to a readable floor (0.80 - see rk-console.ps1). The Height above is
    the size at which that floor is not needed at all and everything is at its
    natural size; the work area of a 1920x1080 screen at 96 DPI is 1920x1032,
    so this is as tall as the window can be and still sit on the desktop.

    MinHeight/MinWidth above are a FLOOR, not the truth. rk-console.ps1 measures
    what the finished layout needs with the chrome at its smallest, and raises
    the two minimums to that - so the window cannot be dragged small enough to
    cut a button off no matter what stage.json is changed to. Because the stage
    no longer gives way, MinWidth is now essentially the stage's own width plus
    the log's, and the pair written above stays smaller than that on purpose - it
    is what a SMALLER stage.json would be allowed to shrink to.

    NO FLOOR NUMBER IS WRITTEN HERE ANY MORE. It is measured at startup and
    printed by -RenderTo, it is DIFFERENT IN EVERY THEME (measured 2026-09-13:
    modern 1746x1026, eve 1748x1003, cyber 1746x1018, pixel 1748x1003), and a
    single number in a comment that nothing checks is a number that lies
    quietly. The one that used to stand here said 1748x991 for all of them.

    A note on what the third top-row button cost, because the first version of
    this paragraph blamed it for the change and that was WRONG - measured by
    A/B, with BtnPanel and its column deleted, the chrome cost table comes back
    byte-identical and the floor is the same 1748x1003. It costs no height at
    all. What it costs is 104px of WIDTH off BtnPower at the 0.80 floor
    (1031 -> 927), which a two-character label does not notice.
  -->
  <Window.Resources>

    <!--THEME-->


    <!-- ==== BELKA'S EYES ==================================================
         The press feedback is her iris, and these three values are not picked
         by eye - they are the hand-written ends of the ramp her iris is built
         from, copied out of ui\stage\gen-eye.py:

             ENDS['run'] = [(0x06,0x2B,0x18), (0x14,0x6B,0x36), (0x2E,0xC4,0x62),
                            (0x8C,0xFF,0xB4), (0xE8,0xFF,0xF2)]

         'run' is the green set, and gen-eye.py says why it gets the most
         shades: "green is the state the window is in almost always". Her eye
         changes colour with the server's state (care = amber, halt = red), so
         there is no single eye colour in the strict sense - the press glow
         takes the one she wears when nothing is wrong, because a red flash
         under the finger would read as an error the press had caused.

         EyeIris is the middle anchor - the body of the iris. Measured across
         all twelve 'run' frames in stage\draw-eye, the most-painted tone is
         #24A050 and the next two are #3BCC6D and #6DEB99, i.e. the ramp either
         side of this anchor; the anchor is the authored value and the others
         are interpolation, so the anchor is what is used.
         EyeGlow is the highlight anchor, EyeInk the darkest.

         The colour these replaced (#5BFF9F) was invented on 2026-09-13 and had
         no source at all, which is the only reason replacing it costs nothing.

         MEASURED, INCLUDING WHERE IT IS WEAKEST. Pressed fill against the fill
         the button already has under the pointer (hover), by luminance:
           eve    0.646 -> 0.409   clear      cyber  0.757 -> 0.409   clear
           pixel  0.663 -> 0.409   clear      modern 0.385 -> 0.409   WEAK
         modern's own accent IS a green (#4F9C74 / hover #6BB68E), so on that
         theme the pressed PRIMARY differs from hover almost only in saturation
         - 1.06:1, and in greyscale the two are indistinguishable. Every
         secondary is fine everywhere (near-black surface -> 0.409). Eva's saved
         theme is eve. Left as is rather than quietly bending the colour: the
         colour is the thing she asked for, and a theme-local override is a
         decision for her, recorded in DECISIONS.md.

         Label on the pressed fill is 6.72:1 in every theme.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EyeIris" Color="#2EC462"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EyeGlow" Color="#8CFFB4"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EyeInk"  Color="#062B18"/>

    <Style x:Key="Eyebrow" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource Body}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12"/>
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource InkSubtle}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="BtnSecondary" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource Body}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Ink}"/>
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource Surface2}"/>
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Hairline}"/>
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderW}"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,7"/>
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="8,0,0,0"/>
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
      <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
            <Border x:Name="bd" CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadBtn}"
                    Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource HairlineStrong}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <!-- Held down. The border goes fluorescent for exactly as long as
                   the button is pressed, because every one of these buttons acts
                   by writing a file and then saying so somewhere ELSE (the log
                   column, the state badge) - a beat later, and somewhere the
                   finger is not. This is the half that answers "did it take my
                   click" at the moment of the click. Colour only: changing the
                   thickness would nudge the label by a pixel on every press. -->
              <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background"  Value="{StaticResource EyeIris}"/>
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource EyeGlow}"/>
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource EyeInk}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource InkSubtle}"/>
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Hairline}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- Same primary button as the panel: the two windows are one program. -->
    <Style x:Key="BtnPrimary" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource OnAccent}"/>
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource Accent}"/>
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Accent}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
            <Border x:Name="bd" CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadBtn}"
                    Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="1">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource AccentHover}"/>
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource AccentHover}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background"  Value="{StaticResource EyeIris}"/>
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource EyeGlow}"/>
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource EyeInk}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource InkSubtle}"/>
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="#191918"/>
                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Hairline}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>


    <!-- SCROLLBARS. Defined once here, because there are two scrolling surfaces
         now: the log, and the tablet - which could not scroll at all until the
         frame around it was given a size, so its scrollbar had never been seen.
         The default WPF one is a light-theme Windows control and reads as a
         hole punched in the page. A hairline track and a thumb that only
         asserts itself on hover - the surface ladder does the hierarchy here,
         same as everywhere else in DESIGN.md.

         KEYED, and re-declared as an implicit style inside each ScrollViewer
         below. A keyless TargetType="ScrollBar" style sitting up here does NOT
         reach them: the scrollbars are built by the ScrollViewer's own control
         template, and a template-generated element does not look for implicit
         styles in the window's resources. Moving this block up here without
         that step put the white Windows scrollbar back in the log, which is
         what it looked like before any of this was themed. -->
    <Style x:Key="ScrollThumb" TargetType="Thumb">
      <Setter Property="OverridesDefaultStyle" Value="True"/>
      <Setter Property="IsTabStop" Value="False"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Thumb">
            <Border x:Name="th" CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadBtn}" Margin="3,0" Background="{StaticResource ScrollThumbBrush}"/>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="th" Property="Background" Value="#4A4844"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsDragging" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="th" Property="Background" Value="#5C5A55"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- No arrow buttons: they are 20 years of habit and nobody clicks them. -->
    <Style x:Key="ScrollBarLook" TargetType="ScrollBar">
      <Setter Property="OverridesDefaultStyle" Value="True"/>
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="10"/>
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="ScrollBar">
            <Grid Background="Transparent">
              <Track x:Name="PART_Track" IsDirectionReversed="True">
                <Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
                  <RepeatButton Command="ScrollBar.PageUpCommand" Opacity="0" Focusable="False"/>
                </Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
                <Track.Thumb>
                  <Thumb Style="{StaticResource ScrollThumb}"/>
                </Track.Thumb>
                <Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
                  <RepeatButton Command="ScrollBar.PageDownCommand" Opacity="0" Focusable="False"/>
                </Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
              </Track>
            </Grid>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- One log line. Monospace, because server logs are columns of timestamps
         and thread names and a proportional font destroys them. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="LogLine">
      <!-- Colour is bound as a STRING, for the same reason the tablet's list
           does it: a SolidColorBrush built in PowerShell arrives as a PSObject
           WPF cannot convert to a Brush, the binding fails without a word, and
           every line renders in the default black - which on this ground is
           invisible. This template bound a Brush from the day it was written
           until 2026-09-09, and the only way it was ever going to be found was
           by measuring the pixels (63,671 of them were #000000). -->
      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Text}" Foreground="{Binding Colour}"
                 FontFamily="{StaticResource Mono}" FontSize="12"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,1"/>
    </DataTemplate>
  </Window.Resources>

  <!--
    LAYOUT. The stage used to be a narrow column beside a wide log. It leads
    now. The window exists to be a face for a server that has none, and a
    240x150 thumbnail beside a wall of text is not a face - the log was reading
    as the window and the keeper as its decoration, which is backwards.

    The log and the command box share the right column on purpose: the log is
    what the server said and the box is what you say back, and that exchange
    belongs in one place. The four buttons live under the stage because they act
    on THIS server - fleet-wide and maintenance actions (quarantine, logscan,
    policy) stay in the panel, which is the window you use to tend servers
    rather than watch one.
  -->
  <!-- The vertical inset is 12 rather than 16: with the stage no longer able to
       give any height back, every band of empty pixels above and below the
       layout is height the buttons would otherwise have had to pay for.

       UseLayoutRounding is repeated here, on the root of the CONTENT, and not
       only on the Window. The stage is 1:1 pixel art and it is only 1:1 if the
       frame lands on whole device pixels - and the header above it is now
       scaled type, whose height is 23.4px as easily as 24. The Window's own
       flag does not reach this grid when the content is detached and rendered
       on its own, which is exactly what -RenderTo does: measured at the
       smallest window size the canvas was being arranged at y=39.6. -->
  <Grid x:Name="RootGrid" Margin="20,12,20,12" UseLayoutRounding="True">
    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
      <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
      <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

    <!-- ================= LEFT: the stage, and this server's four verbs ======= -->
    <Grid x:Name="ColLeft" Grid.Column="0">
      <Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
      </Grid.RowDefinitions>

      <!-- header -->
      <StackPanel Grid.Row="0">
        <!-- Trimmed, and given a MaxWidth in code. Both of these are sentences of
             unknown length (a server label somebody chose, a subtitle with a
             state and a line count in it) sitting in an Auto column, and an Auto
             column is as wide as its widest child - so one long label would
             widen the whole left half of the window and squeeze the log. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="TxtTitle" FontFamily="{StaticResource Display}" FontSize="22"
                   FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{StaticResource Ink}"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   Effect="{StaticResource GlowFx}"/>
        <TextBlock x:Name="TxtSub" Style="{StaticResource Eyebrow}" Margin="0,2,0,0"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- the stage: background layers + whoever is standing on them.
           The Margin here, the one under TxtStageLine, and every margin in the
           button block below are all re-set by rk-console.ps1 from one scale -
           the numbers written in this file are that scale at 1.0. -->
      <Border x:Name="StageCard" Grid.Row="1" Margin="0,8,0,0" Padding="0"
              Background="{StaticResource Surface1}" CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadCard}"
              BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderW}"
              VerticalAlignment="Top" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
        <StackPanel>
          <!-- THE FRAME, and the one thing in this file that must have a size.
               Nothing here says what that size IS - rk-console.ps1 sets it from
               the canvas rk-stage.ps1 built out of stage.json, shrunk to fit the
               window - but the frame having one at all is what makes the tablet
               below work.

               An Auto row inside an Auto column measures its children with
               infinite width AND infinite height, and everything under it
               inherits that. With no edge to find, the tablet's ScrollViewer
               grew instead of scrolling, TextWrapping="Wrap" never wrapped, and
               the close button was arranged thousands of pixels off the right of
               the screen; the swollen column then pushed the buttons and the
               command box out of the window. All three symptoms are the same
               infinity. (2026-09-10)

               ClipToBounds: art is free to be drawn larger than the frame it is
               seen through, which is how a background can pan later without the
               layout changing size. -->
          <Border x:Name="StageFrame" ClipToBounds="True">
            <Grid>
              <!-- The art scales with the frame; the tablet does NOT, which is why
                   it is a sibling of this Viewbox and not inside it. A tablet
                   scaled with the picture would render its text at a fraction of
                   the size it was chosen at. DownOnly because the pixel art has a
                   native zoom already applied (stage.json's scale) and blowing it
                   up past that would only soften it. -->
              <Viewbox Stretch="Uniform" StretchDirection="DownOnly">
                <Canvas x:Name="StageHost"/>
              </Viewbox>

              <!-- THE TABLET. She holds one; pressing a button below makes her
                   hand it over, and what it says lands here, over the stage.

                   It covers her deliberately. A panel that slid in beside her
                   would be a second thing to read while she keeps moving in the
                   corner of the eye; this is the same gesture as being passed a
                   screen - what you were looking at is now the screen. Closing
                   it puts her back. -->
              <Border x:Name="TabletLayer" Visibility="Collapsed"
                      Background="#FF060A12" Padding="16,14"
                      BorderBrush="{StaticResource Accent}" BorderThickness="2">
                <Grid>
                  <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                    <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                  </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                  <Grid Grid.Row="0" Margin="0,0,0,10">
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                      <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                      <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="TxtTabletTitle" Grid.Column="0"
                               FontFamily="{StaticResource Display}" FontSize="16" FontWeight="Bold"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource Accent}" Effect="{StaticResource GlowFx}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    <Button x:Name="BtnTabletClose" Grid.Column="1" Content="✕ とじる"
                            Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="8,0,0,0"/>
                  </Grid>

                  <!-- Disabled, not Hidden: Disabled is what makes the row width
                       finite, which is what makes TextWrapping wrap. It only
                       works because the frame around all of this now has a size
                       - with an infinite width to fall back on there was nothing
                       for "no horizontal scrolling" to mean. -->
                  <ScrollViewer x:Name="TabletScroll" Grid.Row="1" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <ScrollViewer.Resources>
                      <Style TargetType="ScrollBar" BasedOn="{StaticResource ScrollBarLook}"/>
                    </ScrollViewer.Resources>
                    <ItemsControl x:Name="TabletList">
                      <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate>
                          <!-- Colour and FontName are bound as STRINGS. Binding a
                               SolidColorBrush built in PowerShell hands WPF a
                               PSObject it cannot convert to a Brush, so the
                               binding silently fails and every line renders in
                               the default black - invisible on this ground, and
                               only caught by measuring the pixels. A string goes
                               through the type converter and works. -->
                          <TextBlock Text="{Binding Text}" Foreground="{Binding Colour}"
                                     FontFamily="{Binding FontName}" FontSize="{Binding Size}"
                                     TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                        </DataTemplate>
                      </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl>
                  </ScrollViewer>

                  <!-- Only the destructive screen puts anything here, and it is
                       never the default focus. -->
                  <StackPanel Grid.Row="2" x:Name="TabletActions" Orientation="Horizontal"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,10,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed">
                    <TextBox x:Name="TabletInput" Width="260" Visibility="Collapsed"
                             FontFamily="{StaticResource Mono}" FontSize="13"
                             Background="{StaticResource Surface2}" Foreground="{StaticResource Ink}"
                             CaretBrush="{StaticResource Accent}"
                             BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderW}"
                             Padding="8,6" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    <Button x:Name="BtnTabletConfirm" Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}"/>
                  </StackPanel>
                </Grid>
              </Border>
            </Grid>
          </Border>
          <TextBlock x:Name="TxtStageLine" Margin="12,6,12,8"
                     FontFamily="{StaticResource Body}" FontSize="12"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Center"
                     Foreground="{StaticResource InkMuted}"/>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- The four verbs. Start and stop are ONE button: the server is either up
           or it is not, and a pair where one half is always dead is two controls
           carrying one bit. Its label is set from panel-strings.ja.json. -->
      <!-- Power stands alone and full width. Starting and stopping a server
           people are playing on is not the same KIND of act as looking
           something up, and putting it in a grid of eight equals would say it
           was. It also never plays the hand-off animation: a stop must happen
           the instant it is asked for.

           The eight below are what she can be asked to show. They all go
           through the tablet. -->
      <!-- THIS BLOCK IS THE ONE THAT GIVES WAY. Font size, padding and every
           margin below are written by rk-console.ps1 from a single scale that
           follows the window's height; what stands here is that scale at 1.0,
           and Margin="0" on the eight is there only so the style's own
           Margin="8,0,0,0" cannot leak in before the code has spoken. The
           floor is 0.80 - past that the kanji stop being kanji. -->
      <StackPanel x:Name="BtnBlock" Grid.Row="2" Margin="0,8,0,0">
        <!-- Start/stop and the maintenance restart share the top row: the
             power button is the wide one because it is the one pressed daily;
             the restart takes only its own width. Both margins are rewritten
             by Set-RkUiScale, like everything else in this block.

             BtnPanel rides in the same row rather than in a row of its own
             because this block is the one that gives its height away when the
             window shrinks, and a fourth band would come straight out of the
             stage. It is the only control here that does not act on this
             server, which is why it sits at the far edge, apart from the two
             that do. -->
        <Grid x:Name="PowerRow" Margin="0,0,0,6">
          <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
          </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <Button x:Name="BtnPower" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnMaint" Grid.Column="1" Content="メンテ再起動" Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}"
                  Margin="6,0,0,0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnPanel" Grid.Column="2" Content="サーバー一覧へ" Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}"
                  Margin="6,0,0,0"/>
        </Grid>
        <UniformGrid x:Name="BtnGrid" Rows="2" Columns="4">
          <Button x:Name="BtnTabPlayers" Content="オンラインの人" Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnTabVitals"  Content="調子"           Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnReport"     Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}"                          Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnDiagnose"   Content="診断・宿題"     Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnTabCommand" Content="コマンド"       Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnTabAttend"  Content="ねぎらう"       Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnFolder"     Content="フォルダ"       Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="0"/>
          <Button x:Name="BtnTabRemove"  Content="取り外す"       Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}" Margin="0"/>
        </UniformGrid>
      </StackPanel>
    </Grid>

    <!-- ================= RIGHT: what it said, and what you say back ========== -->
    <Grid x:Name="ColRight" Grid.Column="1" Margin="16,0,0,0">
      <Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
      </Grid.RowDefinitions>

      <!-- the log -->
      <Border Grid.Row="0" Padding="12,10"
              Background="{StaticResource Surface1}" CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadCard}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderW}">
      <ScrollViewer x:Name="LogScroll" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                    HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
        <ScrollViewer.Resources>
          <Style TargetType="ScrollBar" BasedOn="{StaticResource ScrollBarLook}"/>
        </ScrollViewer.Resources>
        <ItemsControl x:Name="LogList" ItemTemplate="{StaticResource LogLine}"/>
      </ScrollViewer>
    </Border>

      <!-- the command line -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="1" Margin="0,12,0,0">
      <Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
      </Grid.RowDefinitions>
      <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
      </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

      <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="&gt;" FontFamily="{StaticResource Mono}" FontSize="15"
                 Foreground="{StaticResource Accent}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="2,0,10,0"
                 Effect="{StaticResource GlowFx}"/>

      <TextBox Grid.Column="1" x:Name="TxtCommand"
               FontFamily="{StaticResource Mono}" FontSize="13"
               Background="{StaticResource Surface2}" Foreground="{StaticResource Ink}"
               CaretBrush="{StaticResource Accent}"
               BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderW}"
               Padding="10,8"/>

      <Button Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" x:Name="BtnSend" Content="送信" Style="{StaticResource BtnSecondary}"/>

      <!-- Why the command box is disabled, when it is. An input that is greyed
           out with no reason given reads as a broken window. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="TxtCmdNote" Style="{StaticResource Eyebrow}"
                 Margin="0,8,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
    </Grid>
    </Grid>
  </Grid>
</Window>
